# mozjs -- SpiderMonkey, taken from the Firefox ESR source, and the reason
# GNOME Shell can exist here at all. gnome-shell is written in JavaScript, it
# runs on gjs, and gjs is a binding for this.
#
# WHICH VERSION, AND HOW IT WAS DECIDED. Not by picking the newest ESR:
#
#   * gjs 1.84.2's own meson.build says `spidermonkey = dependency('mozjs-128')`
#     (line 144), unconditional and unguarded;
#   * BLFS 12.4 -- the book README's "Versions" section says the desktop tier
#     follows -- ships that exact gjs and builds it against SpiderMonkey from
#     firefox-140.2.0esr, applying gjs-1.84.2-spidermonkey_140-1.patch to move
#     it off 128.
#
# So the pkg-config name the UNPATCHED gjs demands is mozjs-128 and the one it
# demands after the book's patch is mozjs-140. This tree follows the book, for
# the reason the book is followed at all: the set is known to build together.
# 128 would additionally mean building a mid-2024 ESR with the 1.97 rustc
# packaged here, which is a two-year gap in the direction that historically
# breaks.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env. The
# tarball is the whole browser -- 601 MB, unpacking to firefox-140.2.0 -- and
# only js/src is built out of it. That is upstream's arrangement, not a choice
# available here: SpiderMonkey has had no separate release tarball for years.
SRC_URL=https://archive.mozilla.org/pub/firefox/releases/140.2.0esr/source/firefox-140.2.0esr.source.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=956dce675c3b706d563caf07ed3ca9af632ab830be710dfd4351da78a0a2ef55
SRC_DIR=firefox-140.2.0

# The directory inside the tarball is firefox-140.2.0 and the file is named
# ...140.2.0esr..., which is why SRC_DIR alone would not tell you what this is.
SRC_FILE=firefox-140.2.0esr.source.tar.xz

# gcc, not clang. README's "What is not packaged yet" says of mozjs that it
# needs "clang, which it does not [package] ... turning clang on is most of what
# unblocks mozjs". THAT TURNS OUT NOT TO BE TRUE for a 64-bit build, and it is
# worth correcting rather than working around: upstream's default compiler is
# clang, so BLFS documents CC=gcc CXX=g++ as the way to keep using gcc, and
# notes that clang is "strictly needed" only on 32-bit systems without SSE2 --
# where it exists to stop the x87 unit answering floating-point questions
# differently from the rest of the world. Duct is x86_64 and aarch64, both of
# which use SSE2-equivalent registers by default in every compiler.
#
# So llvm stays as it is, with LLVM_ENABLE_PROJECTS empty, and this package does
# not add a clang build to the tree. That is the difference between a package
# and a package plus an hour of CI on every run.
CC=gcc
CXX=g++
